WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.220
GoDz: ...they've gotta get the throne here. TP in from BurNIng with the boots of travel.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.480
He's got an omnislash and who is he gonna go for? Onto Arteezy.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:07.600
Omnislash comes out...

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.600
Arteezy's gonna get sliced and diced!

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.780
Onto your zai Clockwerk as well! It's a team wipe!

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:14.480
Secret have lost game two.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.480
We're going to a game three.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.020
GoDz: My friends showed me Dota

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.260
I can still remember the guy who I was

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:27.240
kind of playing other random custom
games on Warcraft 3 with. It was initially,

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:33.140
I was living in Singapore, when I was like,
15, 14 years old. I started playing like

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.840
random custom games on Warcraft 3 with some of my school friends, just in

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.309
like LAN cafes after school and stuff
or on weekends, so that got me into

00:00:41.309 --> 00:00:45.921
Warcraft 3, then I kind of got into like
the Warcraft 3 classic melee mode

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.310
and then one day, one of the guys I was
playing with was like "you have to try this

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:52.540
game Dota." I initially hated it, like I
knew it was kind of -

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.560
there was very much this

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.539
just feeling around it, like it was so
popular and big at the time, but it was just

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:01.920
like "oh, it's not like a serious game" or
it's like a... it's kinda like the new..

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.890
I guess, like the way Dota players talk about League was like the way my group

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.670
of friends kind of talked about Dota then
because we all played Warcraft 3, which

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.979
was like the pure game, I guess, and
Dota was just like a spin-off custom

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:12.399
game, so it wasn't to be taken seriously.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.180
They're just going to get the throne off of this!

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.700
Holy sh**!

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:19.120
Cloud9 have lost game two.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.340
I can't believe it!

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.220
Before kind of getting

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:27.621
into the Australian scene as a manager and kind of, I guess... caster, event organizer

00:01:27.621 --> 00:01:31.219
whatever it may be, I was actually playing. I was playing with the Natural 9 guys.

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.540
I played the four position support role for the team,

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:38.800
so I, initially always just wanted to be one
of the pro players.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.740
I was, I guess, that's kind of how
it started for a lot of people, just by

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.880
either playing too much casually for fun
and then getting into casting or playing

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:51.700
in a kind of more serious level, that
was always at least somewhat a dream of mine.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.020
Dennis Gonzales [interviewer]: Why did your pro career not work out?

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.620
GoDz: I was kind of...

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.620
using Dota as...

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:03.259
using my university probably as a cover-up for attempting to play Dota competitively.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:07.610
But, I wasn't really going to classes. I
was kind of struggling, and then, I ended

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:12.400
up moving on and kind of dropping my
studies for the time being, which is when

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.900
I started working and juggling - and that was when Dota 2 came out as well and I was

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:20.690
like "okay I want to make a big push with Dota 2." I started playing with Natural 9

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:23.730
guys, who were the best in Australia, who
ended up actually going to TI2,

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:28.401
so unfortunately, I was juggling trying to
play with them and, at that point, my

00:02:28.401 --> 00:02:32.219
parents had stopped supporting me because I wasn't at university, I wasn't studying

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:37.021
anymore, so I had to have a full-time job,
so I was working in restaurants, bars trying

00:02:37.021 --> 00:02:41.969
to just feed myself... I still remember
a time where I'd like go through all my

00:02:41.969 --> 00:02:45.660
pairs of jeans to just try and find like a
few dollar coins to just like pay for a meal

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.220
or something.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.360
That was what my life was like, at one point, just because

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.980
of how much I just wanted to play
Dota, I wanted to really make it. But I

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:58.940
still remember when the team talked to me and was like "look this isn't gonna work out,

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.030
like you haven't got the time to commit" and in some ways also

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:05.989
just questioned my ability as well and
my ability to commit to the team, and also my

00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:09.530
ability as an individual player.  I
think my performance suffered as a

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:13.040
result and they were like "look we want to try someone else out - we think we can do

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.940
better with him." And that kind of crushed
me, at the time, but obviously not everyone

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:20.090
can be a pro player, there's a lot of
things that get in the way and unfortunately it

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:24.470
didn't work out for me, but it amazingly
led to something else which was just as

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.630
good and challenging in its own ways.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.270
GodZ: I think you're definitely generalizing.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.240
swindlezz: Jesus Christ! I'm gonna stab you with this pen!

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.900
GoDz: Look at TI! Hao suddenly busts out a Luna.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.920
Everyone's like "whoa, Hao's suddenly picking Luna at TI in an elimination match?

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:38.040
And oh, Vici Gaming are winning with Luna."

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.870
swindlezz: I'll give you that. I'll give you that. That's a good point.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:45.660
GoDz: Initially, I was kind of organizing some small... I guess you could call them

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.420
amateur tournaments, but as far as the
Australian scene went it had all the best

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:51.840
teams just because there was like not
much prize money in it, so I would like

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.440
kind of help organize the tournaments
for the teams and people wanted to watch

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.310
it so, I was like, "okay." This is when XSplit and Twitch were getting big, so I

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.190
figured I could just easily make an
account and started streaming the games

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:06.810
and as someone who just had played a ton competitively, loved the game, was just

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:10.350
addicted to the game, to be frank, I was
like "okay, I want to talk about this and

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:15.870
people want to listen," so I just started
going to casting. I ended up joining

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:20.700
Gosu Gamers as one of their main Dota
casters and they gave me a ton of

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.710
support, helped get me to my first LAN
tournament in Thailand as a Dota caster and

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:31.140
about six months to a year later, I
actually got offered my first kind of

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:37.080
esports job in Thailand, where I was
based for about six months...

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.470
GoDz: Hi Kickstarter, I'm David Parker.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.990
LD: And I'm David Gorman.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:43.610
GoDz:  Together, we are Beyond The Summit.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.900
GoDz: Beyond The Summit was kind of
just like a placeholder name for I guess

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.090
the project and initially was maybe a more of a hobby,

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.990
but then it kind of transitioned into this
big kind of project - an idea of mine -

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:59.250
where I wanted to do something to really
help grow the scene and be more involved

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:03.810
myself, but also bring in other people
who had kind of similar goals, ambitions

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:07.620
and LD was someone who had reached out to me and kind of heard some good things about.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:12.240
He had been casting with Luminous
over at Dota Commentary, so I just took a

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:16.350
leap of faith and said "okay, you're from
the US, have a good streaming set-up,

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:20.450
you can stream in like 720p as opposed to the 480p or something we were streaming in

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.830
from Australia" and said "look want to
join me and be the official caster for

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.690
the TI2 Eastern qualifiers?"

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.780
GoDz: ...heroes like Prophet and Morphling. They just cannot stop this push."

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.260
LD: Yeah, this is what we talked
about in the pre-game is WE don't really

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:35.740
want to fight these pushes head on.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:37.460
GoDz: That was, I guess, the first time

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:39.460
I had talked to LD and casted with him.

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:44.040
We hopped into the first cast having never even spoken together and that was just kind

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:47.780
of how things worked at the time. You
just Skype in and say "look,

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.040
we're going to cast together and this is how it's going to work..."

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.900
It was a great, great experience. We
worked really well together and then

00:05:54.900 --> 00:06:00.700
later that year we met at TI2 and
everything went really well from there.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:15.340
GoDz: The Summit 2 was when we had [sighs] all kinds of problems.

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.860
PCs not working, internet not working, power not working, I don't know,

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:22.860
the list goes on. That was like a
disaster of an event for us.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:29.320
We made the resolution like "we're
not working with PC Partner again,

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:32.831
we had like all these PCs provided and like half of them failed. And then,

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:36.520
like luckily - I think Red Bull it was -
bailed us out and brought trucks

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.360
filled with some of their PCs from their
office down and we were like "Thank God,

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:41.040
we can actually run an event now."

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.500
We  had all kinds of... I think even DDoS issues throughout the day.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:49.300
The stream was just a
nightmare, everything about The Summit 2 was just...

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:49.820
[sigh]

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:54.360
It was a rough event. But we made it
through and the teams they came back

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.910
they trusted us that we were gonna fix
everything.

00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:58.900
F***ing never again, man.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:01.700
Never again will we do an event like that.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:04.180
I think by then my parents were fairly accepting

00:07:04.839 --> 00:07:10.749
when I finally relocated for BTS. It was
probably the years preceding that where

00:07:10.749 --> 00:07:17.169
they were very, I guess, non-supportive of
the whole what I was doing.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.200
Largely because they didn't and partly
because they didn't understand it,  but

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.569
largely because also I didn't
communicate what I was doing as something -

00:07:25.569 --> 00:07:29.160
I kind of, I guess, knew they wouldn't
initially accept. So rather than trying

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:32.100
to explain and prove to them like "this
matters a lot to me that I'm gonna do

00:07:32.110 --> 00:07:35.439
this, it's gonna be successful", I said "I'm
just gonna do it and then they'll have

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:38.770
to learn to deal with it." The first
LAN tournament I went to in Thailand, I didn't even

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:42.849
tell them I was going to Thailand and it happened to be when there was a bad storm coming

00:07:42.849 --> 00:07:46.000
coming or something and they found out on Facebook that I was there and they contacted me

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.169
like "holy s***, you're in Thailand!? What the hell are you doing!?  Are you okay?

00:07:50.169 --> 00:07:57.520
Are you safe?" So it was... I was
immature, at the time, so

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:02.919
in hindsight that was probably not the best way of approaching it, but I'm really

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:04.460
happy where we are now.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:06.460
My parents... when I first...

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:09.660
The first TI I went to in 2012, 
 my Mum was there.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:13.820
I told her, just said "come, come and see what this is and that

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.600
was around the time where they knew I
was at least somewhat serious about what

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:21.820
I was doing and it was starting to
kind of build-up and become a thing that

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:26.379
I could do more consistently - I was
getting paid to do it.  So, my Mum saw me

00:08:26.389 --> 00:08:30.560
casting or on the panel there in front
of thousands of people at Benaroya Hall

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:34.580
and she was kind of finally had, I think
bit of a realization, like "okay, this

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.539
is not just... competitive gaming is a thing." Like up until that point, my parents

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:40.640
had no idea what it was, they just knew

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:44.270
I'm broadcasting online and people
watching and maybe I make some ad

00:08:44.270 --> 00:08:48.620
revenue. They at least understood that
this is what I was gonna do, so whether I

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:53.870
was going to succeed or not? I didn't
even know, I guess. I was hopeful,

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:57.620
I knew I wasn't going to be a complete failure, but I know if this was something

00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:03.140
that was going to last one year, five years, ten years... And, I think from my parents' point-of-view,

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:06.950
they had the same feelings, but
they were just like "well, he's gonna do

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:11.660
it" and they were supportive, at that point.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:16.540
GoDz: Next for me? Let's see, where do I want to go?

00:09:16.550 --> 00:09:21.950
I'd love to kind of be able to
dedicate more time to personal growth,

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:28.250
as an individual outside of Dota,
I feel like so much of what's defined me

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:33.180
over the last two years has just been Dota related, but also, I do want to grow as a caster, as an analyst.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:36.180
I feel like there's a lot
more I can learn about the game and do

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:40.300
within the game, so I want to challenge
myself, but I also want to make sure that I

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:47.560
continue to find that balance, so that I
can also, I guess,

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:51.410
maintain a decent life balance and then
be able to be more productive and make

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:54.410
sure that events continue to go well
for us.

